<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sechele I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1812–<a href="/years/1892">1892</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sechele I was chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakwena">Bakwena</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tswana-people">Tswana people</a> of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>, from the 1830s until his death in 1892. He was baptised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">David Livingstone</a> in 1848, the missionary's only recorded convert, and later preached as a Christian in his own right. He survived an attack by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> on his town at Dimawe in 1852, made the Kwena a regional power and settled his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molepolole">Molepolole</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sechele-i.jpg" alt="Sechele I" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gustav Theodor Fritsch (1838–1927) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sechele_Gustav_Fritsch_1865.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sechele, also known as Setshele, was the son of the chief of the Kwêna. When he was ten his father was killed and the chiefship was divided between two of his uncles. Sechele fled into the desert with some supporters and spent years among the Ngwato, where he married Mokgokong, a daughter of Chief Kgari. In about 1831 he displaced one of his uncles as ruler of half the Kwêna.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He met Livingstone at Tshwane in 1847 and moved with his people to the Kolobeng River, where the missionary set up the Kolobeng Mission. He learned the alphabet in two days, taught his wives to read from the Tswana Bible and sent five of his children to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-moffat">Robert Moffat</a> at Kuruman. Before baptism he gave up his role as rainmaker and, at Livingstone's insistence, divorced four of his five wives. When one former wife later became pregnant by him, Livingstone denounced him, though Sechele declared that he would never give up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>.</p><p>On Livingstone's urging he sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> to the uncle who ruled the other half of the Kwêna; the suspicious uncle set fire to it and died in the explosion, which let Sechele reunite the people. In 1852, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahurutshe">Bahurutshe</a> who had been enslaved by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> fled to him, the Boers destroyed Kolobeng and attacked Dimawe. Led by Sechele, Khama of the Bangwato and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bathoen-i">Bathoen I</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangwaketse">Bangwaketse</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tswana-people">Batswana</a> defeated them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, then negotiating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sand-river-convention">Sand River Convention</a>, offered only friendly remonstrances, and Sechele's journey to seek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>'s protection ended at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> when his money ran out.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Sechele travelled hundreds of miles to preach to other peoples; when Moffat reached Matabeleland in 1859, he found that Sechele had been there first and that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> held Christian prayers. He later returned to rainmaking and polygamy, and missionaries called him half Christian, half heathen. The historian Neil Parsons judged that he did more to spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in nineteenth-century southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> than virtually any European missionary. His land became a refuge, and he ruled more than 30,000 people when he died.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sechele I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Sechele I</b> rebuilt the chiefdom from the 1830s, ruling through the kgotla and ward system.</blockquote><a href="/state/bakwena-chiefdom">Bakwena Chiefdom</a></li></ul></section>
